<!--
    Type campaign, item `charptr`: a pointer the program only ever uses on
    characters is declared as an integer or as `void *`, although the fact is
    already on the table.  coreutils `realpath` -O0 shows it, on
    `path_prefix(char const *prefix, char const *path)`:

        bool sub_2bbc(void *a0,void *a1)

    and the body walks both arguments one byte at a time.

    Root cause (P5, type propagation).  `TypeOpCall::getInputLocal`
    (typeop.cc:689) states a callee's declared parameter type about the Varnode
    that IS the call's operand, and `propagateTypeEdge` moves a Varnode only to
    a MORE specific type.  Every hop between the value a reader sees declared
    and the use that says "characters" - an -O0 spill through the frame, a
    MULTIEQUAL, a `p + k` whose INT_ADD refuses to carry a pointer back
    (typeop.cc:1217, the same refusal `ptrfromuse` documents) - is a hop the
    fact does not cross.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation".  The P0 assertion
    `option charptr on|off` decides whether character evidence may commit a
    pointer-width parameter or stack local to `char *`.

    The witness is that function itself, 197 bytes of `coreutils realpath -O0`
    0x2bbc copied in whole, plus six controls:

        pathprefix: the witness - `path_prefix(char const *, char const *)`,
                    whose two parameters are spilled to the frame and then
                    walked in place one byte at a time.  Off it is
                    `bool pathprefix(void *a0,void *a1)`
        scaled:     shl rdi,2; call slen
                    a control - RDI is SHIFTED before it reaches the declared
                    `char *`, which no pointer survives
        offsetcall: add rdi,0x13; call slen    where `char *slen(char *)`
                    a control - a declared `char *` reached at a FIXED non-zero
                    offset is about the FIELD there, not about the base, exactly
                    as a one-byte read at a fixed offset is.  cronie `crond` -O0
                    `sub_6715` is the corpus case: `strcmp(base + 0x13, ...)`
                    reads `d_name` out of a `struct dirent`, and 0x13 is
                    `offsetof(struct dirent, d_name)` on x86-64
        fieldbyte:  movzx eax, byte [rdi+0x3c]
                    a control - a one-byte read at a FIXED offset is a struct
                    field, not a character.  coreutils `ginstall`'s
                    `announce_mkdir(char *dir, void *options)` reads the `bool`
                    at `options + 0x3c`, and typing that `char *` would replace
                    a correct `void *` with a wrong claim
        mixedwidth: movzx eax, byte [rdi]; add eax,[rdi+8]
                    a control - the same value is also read four bytes wide, so
                    the byte read does not establish the element type
        otherproto: add rdi,1; call ilen    where `int8 ilen(int8 *)`
                    a control - the callee declares a pointer at something else,
                    which contradicts a character pointer rather than
                    supporting one

    `prefixcmp` is the same byte-at-a-time compare written out by hand, and it
    is here as a SEVENTH control: kuna already types it `char *` without the
    option, through the PTRADD the pointer-arithmetic pool builds, and it must
    not move either way.

    Pass 1 (`option charptr off`) reproduces the bug; pass 2 (`on`) is the fix.
    All seven controls are identical in both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="offsetcall"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100020" name="scaled"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100040" name="prefixcmp"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000c0" name="fieldbyte"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000e0" name="mixedwidth"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100100" name="otherproto"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100140" name="slen"/>
